  "response": "Here's a Python function that reverses the order of elements in a list while keeping each element paired with its corresponding punctuation mark from a separate list. Both lists are reversed in parallel, ensuring alignment is preserved:\n\n```python\ndef reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of elements and their associated punctuation marks.\n\n    Args:\n        elements (list): List of strings representing the main elements.\n        punctuation (list): List of strings representing punctuation marks for each element.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple containing two lists:\n            - new_elements: Reversed list of elements.\n            - new_punctuation: Reversed list of punctuation marks corresponding to elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    return (elements[::-1], punctuation[::-1])\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n\n```python\n# Basic example\nresult = reverse_list_elements([\"Hello\", \"world\"], [\"\", \"!\"])\nprint(result)\n# Output: (['world', 'Hello'], ['!', ''])\n\n# Another example with different punctuation\nelements = [\"Hi\", \"Welcome\"]\npunctuations = [\".\", \"?\"]\nresult = reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuations)\nprint(result)\n# Output: (['Welcome', 'Hi'], ['?', '.'])\n```\n\nThis function returns both the reversed elements and their corresponding punctuation marks in matching order. Note that each element is treated as a string, and punctuation remains separate from it—there's no attempt to attach punctuation characters directly to the text content of the elements themselves.",
